digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56413-9
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate a next-generation programmable quantum current generator that integrates a three-terminal-connected quantum Hall resistance standard with Josephson-voltage driving to realize stand-alone, Josephson-frequency-driven quantized ampere currents without classical correction, spanning microampere to milliampere levels with relative uncertainties below 1e-8.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Limitations of direct current-source approaches and correction-limited interconnections motivate using Josephson voltage and quantum Hall resistance standards with improved QHRS connection geometry to realize accurate, practical quantum currents."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Because the post-2019 ampere is based on the fixed elementary charge and any current expressible as I = e·f realizes the ampere, frequency-linked charge-transfer currents constitute a valid SI realization route for current."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) The work demonstrates ampere realization with relative uncertainty below 1e-8 across different current levels using full quantum instrumentation."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) If non-ideal connections require correction measurements that impair final uncertainty and simplicity, then reducing connection-related correction needs is a justified design objective for PQCG improvement."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Although single-electron current sources are an obvious direct route to the ampere definition, their difficulty in producing currents above 100 pA with relative uncertainty below 1e-7 motivates consideration of alternative quantum-current realization routes."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because electromagnetic-environment coupling can degrade charge-step flatness and accuracy, debated flatness in dual-Shapiro-step current sources means they require further accuracy validation before serving as uncorrected primary current standards."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) A natural quantum-current realization route is to use Ohm’s law with Josephson voltage and quantum Hall resistance standards to generate a quantum-accurate current."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) When a quantized Josephson voltage accurately drives a quantized quantum Hall resistance, the resulting calculable quantized current can reach the microampere range."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) In previous PQCG operation, corrections caused by non-ideal connections impaired uncertainty and simplicity, so reducing or eliminating such correction needs is important for a simpler, lower-uncertainty quantum current standard."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) For a next-generation PQCG aiming at lower-noise Josephson-frequency-driven quantized currents without classical correction, implementing a three-terminal QHRS connection is the relevant design strategy for accurately applying Josephson voltage to the quantized resistance."];
  "n_imp_calc_i" [label="(0,0,0) If a QHRS of quantized resistance is driven by an accurately applied Josephson-quantized voltage, then the resulting current is calculable and quantized (traceable to h and e via V and R)."];
  "n_imp_env" [label="(0,0,0) If a mesoscopic current source couples to an electromagnetic environment, then charge/phase fluctuations can degrade step flatness/accuracy."];
  "n_imp_ohm" [label="(0,0,0) If V is quantum-accurate (Josephson) and R is quantum-accurate (QHE), then using Ohm’s law I=V/R can generate a quantum-accurate current."];
  "n_imp_true_source" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given the 1 μA demonstration depended on a special low-resistance ammeter and accurate series connection is the main issue, therefore the key problem is an interconnection that yields a true stand-alone current source."];
  "n_r2_6" [label="(1,2,6) Currently, since 20 May 2019, the SI definition of the ampere is based on fixing the elementary charge e."];
  "n_s1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, after the 2019 SI revision, any current source whose output can be written as I = e·f realizes the ampere."];
  "n_s10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, with dc bias and microwave frequency f_J, each JJ transfers one fluxon per period, producing quantized voltage V = n_J·Φ0·f_J."];
  "n_s12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, therefore a current I = n_J·e·f_J can be generated, easily reaching microamperes."];
  "n_s13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, 1 μA from series-connected quantum standards was measured with 1.3×10^-7 uncertainty, but that accuracy relied on a very low-resistance ammeter (~0.1 mΩ)."];
  "n_s14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, the main issue is implementing an accurate series connection of the two quantum standards while realizing a true current source."];
  "n_s17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, that PQCG result required corrections of a few parts in 1e7 due to non-ideal connections, determined via time-consuming extra measurements."];
  "n_s18" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, these corrections impair final uncertainty and simplicity, limiting the potential of the quantum current standard."];
  "n_s19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, the proposed next-generation PQCG aims to deliver lower-noise currents at theoretical Josephson-frequency-driven quantized values, without any classical correction."];
  "n_s2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, SECS can transfer electrons one-by-one at rate f_e and are viewed as an obvious way to realize the ampere definition."];
  "n_s20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, this performance is obtained by implementing a three-terminal connection of the QHRS that enables highly accurate application of Josephson voltage to the quantized resistance."];
  "n_s21" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, the work demonstrates ampere realization with relative uncertainty <1e-8 across different current levels, filling the gap between microampere and milliampere ranges."];
  "n_s22" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, this is achieved using full quantum instrumentation with five quantum devices, enabling several electrical units to be realized in one experiment."];
  "n_s3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, for GaAs/Si tunable-barrier SECS, producing >100 pA with relative uncertainty <1e-7 is very challenging because error rates increase at ~1 GHz."];
  "n_s6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, sharp current steps at integer multiples of 2e·f_e could be promising nanoampere-range quantum current sources, but their step flatness is debated."];
  "n_s8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, another SI-realization route is to apply Ohm’s law using Josephson voltage and quantum Hall resistance standards, which realize volt and ohm with ~1e-9 uncertainty."];
  "n_s9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, Josephson voltage standards are series arrays of JJs whose high accuracy relies on macroscopic superconducting phase rigidity."];
